  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Hilbert Spaces [S+N]\n\nA **Hilbert space** is a complete inner product space — that is, a vector space $H$ with a sesquilinear (or bilinear, in real case) inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ satisfying:\n\n1. $\\langle x, x \\rangle \\ge 0$ with equality iff $x = 0$.\n2. $\\langle x, y \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle y, x \\rangle}$.\n3. Linearity in the first argument.\n\nThe induced norm $\\|x\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle x, x \\rangle}$ makes $H$ a Banach space, with extra structure: angles, orthogonality, projections.\n\nThe two prototypes: $\\ell^2$ (square-summable sequences) and $L^2(X, \\mu)$. Both are Hilbert spaces. We saw the orthonormal Fourier basis of $L^2([0,1])$ in Stage 4/05.\n\nThis notebook walks the geometry that comes for free in Hilbert spaces.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Cauchy-Schwarz and the parallelogram law\n\n**Cauchy-Schwarz**: $|\\langle x, y \\rangle| \\le \\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\|$, equality iff $x, y$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Parallelogram law**: $\\|x + y\\|^2 + \\|x - y\\|^2 = 2\\|x\\|^2 + 2\\|y\\|^2$.\n\nThe parallelogram law actually **characterises** Hilbert spaces: a Banach space is Hilbert iff its norm satisfies the parallelogram law. (Not every $L^p$ has this — only $L^2$.)\n\n## Orthogonal projection — the key Hilbert-space construction\n\nLet $M$ be a *closed* subspace of $H$. For every $x \\in H$, there's a unique decomposition\n\n$$x = P_M x + (x - P_M x), \\qquad P_M x \\in M, \\quad (x - P_M x) \\perp M.$$\n\n$P_M x$ is the **orthogonal projection** of $x$ onto $M$ — the closest point in $M$ to $x$. It minimises $\\|x - y\\|$ over $y \\in M$.\n\n**Best approximation theorem**: $\\|x - P_M x\\| = \\min_{y \\in M} \\|x - y\\|$.\n\nIn $\\ell^2$, projection onto the first $N$ coordinates is just truncation. In $L^2$, projection onto the span of an orthonormal set is given by the Fourier coefficients.\n\n## Fourier series in $L^2([0, 1])$\n\nThe set $\\{e_n(x) = e^{2\\pi i n x}\\}_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}}$ is an orthonormal basis of $L^2([0, 1])$. Every $f \\in L^2$ has a unique expansion\n\n$$f = \\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} \\hat f(n)\\,e_n, \\qquad \\hat f(n) = \\langle f, e_n \\rangle = \\int_0^1 f(x)\\,e^{-2\\pi i n x}\\,dx.$$\n\nThe series converges *in $L^2$ norm* (not necessarily pointwise — Carleson's theorem says it converges a.e. for $L^2$ functions, but that's much harder).\n\n**Parseval's identity**: $\\|f\\|_2^2 = \\sum |\\hat f(n)|^2$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Compute Fourier coefficients of a triangle wave on [0, 1] symbolically */\nf_tri : if x < 1/2 then x else 1 - x$  /* triangle peak at 1/2 */\n/* Real Fourier series — symmetric around x = 1/2, so cosine coefficients only */\na_n_expr : 2 * integrate(2*x * cos(2*%pi*n*x), x, 0, 1/2)$\nprint(\"Fourier cosine coefficient a_n for triangle wave:\")$\nratsimp(a_n_expr);",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Fourier cosine coefficient a_n for triangle wave:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "{{\\pi\\,n\\,\\sin \\left(\\pi\\,n\\right)+\\cos \\left(\\pi\\,n\\right)-1}\\over{\\pi^2\\,n^2}}"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 5,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Plot the partial Fourier sums of a triangle wave — illustrating $L^2$ convergence.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Build partial sum of cosine Fourier series for the triangle wave */\nfourier_partial(N) := block([series, n],\n  series : 1.0/4.0,  /* a_0/2 = average value */\n  for n : 1 thru N do block([a],\n    /* Triangle wave Fourier series: a_n = -2(1 - cos(πn))/(π²n²) */\n    a : -2 * (1 - cos(float(%pi)*n)) / (float(%pi)^2 * n^2),\n    series : series + a * cos(2 * float(%pi) * n * x)),\n  series)$\n\nxs : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 1, 500))$\ntrue_tri : map(lambda([xv], if xv < 0.5 then float(xv) else float(1 - xv)), xs)$\nP3 : map(lambda([xv], float(subst(x = xv, fourier_partial(3)))), xs)$\nP10 : map(lambda([xv], float(subst(x = xv, fourier_partial(10)))), xs)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"black\", line_width=2, name=\"triangle f(x)\",\n  lines(xs, true_tri),\n  color=\"red\", line_width=1, name=\"N = 3 partial sum\",\n  lines(xs, P3),\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=1, name=\"N = 10 partial sum\",\n  lines(xs, P10),\n  title=\"L² Fourier convergence of triangle wave\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"value\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
